commit 945b2631889a4d5a462a6df8718d4f806a1b5cbd
Author: lora-dataset-studio <noreply@lora-dataset-studio.dev>
Date:   Wed Aug 5 08:22:08 2026 +0200

    feat(bank): captions remember who wrote them, so Re-caption stops destroying yours
    
    Re-caption warned, honestly, that it could not tell a caption you typed from one
    a model produced: one Text column, one string, no author. That warning was the
    right thing to say and the wrong thing to do.
    
    Three additive columns record it — bank_image.caption_origin,
    face_dataset_image.caption_origin and .caption_short_origin — with no invented
    vocabulary: 'asserted' is face_cluster_origin's token and keeps its contract (the
    pass SKIPS those rows), 'joycaption'/'ollama' is watermark_source's idea (record
    which engine decided, because a bank is captioned over weeks and 'auto' writes
    with both inside one run). NULL is the third state and the important one: origin
    never recorded. Those rows ARE re-captioned — sparing them would make the button
    inert on every bank that exists today — but they are counted apart from the
    machine-written ones, because calling them generated would be an attribution
    nobody measured.
    
    Every writer goes through one helper (services/caption_origin.py) rather than a
    second line next to each assignment: the caption editor, find/replace and .txt
    sidecar imports stamp 'asserted'; the four captioning passes stamp the engine
    that actually wrote the row; clear_caption, regenerate and the concept pass's
    deliberate blanking clear the stamp with the text, so no row stays protected on
    an empty string. The stamp travels every copy path — dataset→bank (the case that
    used to launder hand-written work into an anonymous string), bank→bank, promotion
    back to a dataset, the improve candidate, both re-improve case() updates, and
    _BACKUP_IMG_FIELDS, without which one backup restore would drop every protection
    in silence.
    
    The button now states three things instead of two: what it rewrites (and that is
    the number of rows that change — pile minus what it spares), what it keeps
    because you wrote it, and what it overwrites whose author was never recorded. The
    way out of the protection is a separate tick box, offered only when there is
    something to protect, never pre-ticked, named again in the confirmation, and sent
    as a key that is simply absent by default.
    
    Tests assert WHICH ROWS changed, not which arguments transited: one bank holding
    machine, hand-written, unrecorded and rejected captions, forced once, checked row
    by row; the announced count asserted against the rows that really moved; the
    three copy paths; a legacy SQLite database gaining the columns with its old rows
    staying NULL; and the unforced pass proven byte-identical.
